.TXT 3 1 1 0 0
Cg a73.000000,73.000000,565
{\rtf\ansi \deff0{\colortbl;\red0\green0\blue255;}{\fonttbl{\f0
\fcharset0\fnil Arial;}}\plain\cf1\fs20 \pard {\b Project 2.2}  The 
direct computation of (2.2) or (2.3) is often not a preferred 
computation, as we will soon discuss. However, if M<<N then they can 
be an attractive computation, especially if we have a restricted 
computational ability in the processor. There are simple FIR filters 
that are useful: we consider some here. For each filter, see if you 
can describe its operation. One way to do this is to filter some trial 
input sequences- not necessarily restricted to a (single) sinusoid! We 
give the nonzero values of each FIR which is zero-padded out to length N.}
.TXT 14 2 3 0 0
Cg a71.000000,71.000000,108
{\rtf\ansi \deff0{\colortbl;\red0\green0\blue255;}{\fonttbl{\f0
\fcharset0\fnil Arial;}}\plain\cf1\fs20 \pard Hint: the filters 
include low, band and high pass filters and approximations to certain 
calculus operations.}
.TXT 6 3 2 0 0
Cg a69.000000,69.000000,17
{\rtf\ansi \deff0{\colortbl;\red0\green0\blue255;}{\fonttbl{\f0
\fcharset0\fnil Arial;}}\plain\cf1\fs20 \pard (a)  M=0,  a{\fs16\dn 0} 
= 1}
.TXT 0 53 9 0 0
